0.13150915503501892,0.0,-7.510157047363464e-06,-0.7353336811065674,0.0,-0.00021443451987579465,0.0,-8.856858039507642e-05,0.0,0.0,-9.536738616588991e-07,0.0,-2.3841855067985307e-07,-9.536738616588991e-07,0.0,-3.576278118089249e-07,-1.1920928244535389e-07,0.0,-1.5497195136049413e-06,-2.9802276912960224e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,10061,57960,10374,13233,90,64,2137,3,57960,10374,13233,90,65,2137,3,323,57960,10374,13233,90,66,31716,387,2326,4982,22879,11,1741,429,279,9210,1948,894,315,1105,374,279,29783,9210,57960,15976,2418,220,576,8123,315,279,27853,32507,291,2248,7907,553,1493,2326,22879,374,57960,37018,90,16,15170,59,26888,90,18,21,15,3417,2418,220,7379,198,78045,18,1124,9407,61,17,1124,15976,481,220,17,1124,9407,61,18,1124,15976,7110,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ be three unit vectors, such that the angle between any of them is the acute angle $\\theta.$  The volume of the tetrahedron generated by these three vectors is $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{360}}.$  Find\n\\[3 \\cos^2 \\theta - 2 \\cos^3 \\theta.\\]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"50a23365e77b7b315009d3f47fd85e1dc6a5cfe6a69ce0b811c9d95880bce246"},"seconds":83.1230685710907,"seed":1106352651193362996,"stop":"eos","text":"To find \\(3 \\cos^2 \\theta - 2 \\cos^3 \\theta\\), we start by using the information given about the volume of the tetrahedron formed by the vectors \\(\\mathbf{a}\\), \\(\\mathbf{b}\\), and \\(\\mathbf{c}\\). The volume \\(V\\) of a tetrahedron formed by vectors \\(\\mathbf{a}\\), \\(\\mathbf{b}\\), and \\(\\mathbf{c}\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{6} \\left| \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) \\right|\n\\]\n\nGiven that the volume is \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{360}}\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{6} \\left| \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) \\right| = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{360}}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying both sides by 6, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\left| \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) \\right| = \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{360}} = \\frac{6}{6\\sqrt{10}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\mathbf{a}\\), \\(\\mathbf{b}\\), and \\(\\mathbf{c}\\) are unit vectors and the angle between any two of them is \\(\\theta\\), the magnitude of the cross product \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\|\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}\\| = \\|\\mathbf{b}\\| \\|\\mathbf{c}\\| \\sin \\theta = \\sin \\theta\n\\]\n\nThus, the dot product \\(\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})\\) can be expressed as:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = \\|\\mathbf{a}\\| \\|\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}\\| \\cos \\phi = \\sin \\theta \\cos \\phi\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\phi\\) is the angle between \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}\\). Since \\(\\mathbf{a}\\), \\(\\mathbf{b}\\), and \\(\\mathbf{c}\\) are unit vectors and the angle between any two of them is \\(\\theta\\), the angle \\(\\phi\\) is \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\theta\\), so \\(\\cos \\phi = \\sin \\theta\\). Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = \\sin \\theta \\sin \\theta = \\sin^2 \\theta\n\\]\n\nThus, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\sin^2 \\theta = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sin^2 \\theta = 1 - \\cos^2 \\theta\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n1 - \\cos^2 \\theta = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(x = \\cos^2 \\theta\\). Then:\n\n\\[\n1 - x = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\implies x = 1 - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{10} - 1}{\\sqrt{10}}\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\(3 \\cos^2 \\theta - 2 \\cos^3 \\theta\\). Let \\(y = \\cos \\theta\\), so \\(y^2 = x\\). We need to find \\(3y^2 - 2y^3\\). Since \\(y^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{10} - 1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\), we have:\n\n\\[\ny = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\sqrt{10} - 1}{\\sqrt{10}}}\n\\]\n\nThen:\n\n\\[\n3y^2 - 2y^3 = 3 \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{10} - 1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\right) - 2 \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{10} - 1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\right)^{3/2}\n\\]\n\nSince the problem only asks for the value of \\(3 \\cos^2 \\theta - 2 \\cos^3 \\theta\\) and we know \\(y^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{10} - 1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\), we can simplify directly:\n\n\\[\n3 \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{10} - 1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\right) - 2 \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{10} - 1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\right)^{3/2} = \\frac{3(\\sqrt{10} - 1)}{\\sqrt{10}} - 2 \\left( \\frac{(\\sqrt{10} - 1)^{3/2}}{\\sqrt{10}^{3/2}} \\right) = \\frac{3(\\sqrt{10} - 1)}{\\sqrt{10}} - \\frac{2(\\sqrt{10} - 1)^{3/2}}{10^{3/4}}\n\\]\n\nAfter simplifying, we get:\n\n\\[\n3 \\cos^2 \\theta - 2 \\cos^3 \\theta = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
